== Parts adapted from other projects ==

The way MultiPak runs snaps follows snapd's snap-confine, snap-exec and interface
rules: the list of host directories shown to a strictly confined snap, the NVIDIA
library globs, the layout rules, the environment and command assembly, the mounts of
the desktop interfaces, the order of hooks, snapctl's output formats, where components
are shown, and the D-Bus services each interface uses.
https://github.com/canonical/snapd
Canonical Ltd. and snapd contributors. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only.

Android APK bundle inspection and split classification in
src/multipak/android_bundle.py are adapted from App Manager's ApkFile.java:
https://github.com/MuntashirAkon/AppManager/blob/master/app/src/main/java/io/github/muntashirakon/AppManager/apk/ApkFile.java
App Manager contributors. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later.

ARM native-bridge overlay layout and configuration properties in
src/multipak/android_translation_helper.py follow Waydroid Extras Script:
https://github.com/casualsnek/waydroid_script/blob/48dbfaf34a6ddbe78688c530f9ba1c26522aafb2/stuff/ndk.py
Waydroid Extras Script contributors. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later.
Proprietary native-bridge binaries are not included in MultiPak.

== Downloaded when needed ==

Container images are not bundled. MultiPak uses Distrobox and Podman to download the
official images of Fedora, Ubuntu, Debian, Arch Linux, openSUSE, Mageia, ALT Linux, Void
Linux, Alpine Linux, Gentoo and Pisi Linux from their registries (quay.io, docker.io,
ghcr.io). These components keep their own licenses.

The Android system (LineageOS) is not bundled. When an Android container is created,
MultiPak downloads the LineageOS system and vendor images for Waydroid from
https://ota.waydro.id/. These components keep their own licenses:
https://lineageos.org/
Android is a trademark of Google LLC.

== Downloaded when needed, with extra steps ==

Slackware is not bundled. MultiPak uses an unofficial Slackware 15.0 image
(docker.io/aclemons/slackware, https://github.com/aclemons/slackware-dockerfiles) and installs
official Slackware packages in it, checked with the Slackware key above. These components keep
their own licenses: http://www.slackware.com/

SliTaz GNU/Linux is not bundled. When a SliTaz package is installed for the first time,
MultiPak downloads the official SliTaz Base ISO and a few packages (GNU Wget, CA
certificates and their dependencies) from https://mirror.slitaz.org/ and builds a
local container image from them on the user's computer. These components keep their
own licenses: https://www.slitaz.org/

Gentoo Linux is not bundled. When a Gentoo package is installed for the first time,
MultiPak downloads Gentoo's official base system image (docker.io/gentoo/stage3), the
Portage package tree and Gentoo's official binary packages from
https://distfiles.gentoo.org/. These components keep their own licenses:
https://www.gentoo.org/

Puppy Linux is not bundled. MultiPak converts a .pet package chosen by the user into a
Slackware or Debian package on the user's computer. To find the libraries such a
package needs in a Debian or Ubuntu environment, MultiPak installs apt-file (GPL-2.0-or-later)
from that distribution's repository, which downloads the repository's file index.

Alpine Linux is not bundled. When an Alpine package is installed for the first time,
MultiPak downloads Alpine's official image for the chosen release
(docker.io/library/alpine) and the package's dependencies from Alpine's repositories,
and adds Alpine's translation packages ("lang") and musl-locales (MIT). These components keep their own licenses:
https://www.alpinelinux.org/

Solus is not bundled. When a Solus package is installed for the first time, MultiPak
downloads an unofficial image built in public CI from Solus's official repository
(docker.io/silkeh/solus:base, https://gitlab.com/silkeh/docker-solus) and builds a small
local image from it on the user's computer, adding the user database and os-release
links from the image's own baselayout files. Packages come from Solus's repository.
These components keep their own licenses: https://getsol.us/
